+/*
+ * laurel 2101: screen-surface pool. The window server's screen-sized surfaces (720x1560 BGRA, 4.8 MB; with the
+ * iPhone X canvas of sweetfb_native=0 1125x2436, 11 MB) go to
+ * the MDP scanout path, which needs physically contiguous memory below 4 GiB. Taken per surface, late in
+ * the boot, that failed on every try (boot 2026-11-01 04:04: vm_page_find_contiguous(num=1172) found 0
+ * pages, "backboardd create id 0 720x1560 ... -> 0xe00002bd" in a loop, no frame). So the buffers are
+ * set aside once, when IOCoreSurfaceRoot starts (before user space runs): LAUREL_SPOOL_COUNT (= the
+ * scanout slots) of LAUREL_SPOOL_SIZE, contiguous, 16 KiB aligned (user tasks have 16 KiB pages), below
+ * 4 GiB. A buffer goes back when its surface is freed; it is handed out again only when nobody else (a
+ * task's mapping, the framebuffer) holds it any more. When all are in use, the surface gets wired pages
+ * (laurel_surf_pages, copy path) at once: no scan of the whole RAM for a contiguous run under the VM lock.
+ * Kill switch laurelspool=0. One line at start (with the RAM below 4 GiB still free after the fill; laurel
+ * 2100 keeps it for last):
+ *   "LAUREL SPOOL: on: 6 x 12 MiB contiguous below 4 GiB at 0x..-0x.. for screen surfaces; below-4G free N MiB"
+ *   "LAUREL SPOOL: off: <reason>" (the scanout path then allocates per surface, as before)
